CLIENT-SIDE STORAGE IN WEB BROWSERS
(localStorage, sessionStorage, Cookies)

All three are client-side storage mechanisms.
This means the data is stored in the users browser, not on the server, not in a database, and not secure by default.

Cookies came first.
localStorage and sessionStorage were created later to solve cookie limitations.

────────────────────────────────

COOKIES

What cookies are:
Cookies are small pieces of text data stored in the browser and automatically sent to the server with every HTTP request.

This automatic sending is the main reason cookies still exist.

Size limit:
Approximately 4 KB per cookie.
Very small. Not suitable for large data.

Lifetime:
Cookies can be:

* Session cookies: deleted when the browser closes
* Persistent cookies: expire at a specific date

Example:
Set-Cookie: token=abc123; expires=Fri, 31 Dec 2026

Accessibility:

* Accessible by the server
* Accessible by JavaScript unless marked HttpOnly

JavaScript access:
document.cookie
Returns a single string, not an object.

Security flags:
HttpOnly  -> JavaScript cannot access the cookie (prevents XSS)
Secure   -> Sent only over HTTPS
SameSite -> Helps prevent CSRF attacks

localStorage and sessionStorage do NOT have these security features.

Typical cookie use cases:

* Authentication sessions
* Login tokens
* User tracking
* Server-side preferences (language, region)

Disadvantages:

* Sent with every request (performance cost)
* Very small storage size
* Messy API
* Security risks if misused

Key point:
If data must be sent automatically to the server, cookies are the only option.

────────────────────────────────

LOCALSTORAGE

What localStorage is:
A browser-based key–value storage that persists even after the browser is closed.

Data is NOT automatically sent to the server.

Size limit:
Approximately 5–10 MB per domain.

Lifetime:
Permanent.
Data stays until:

* Explicitly removed by code
* User clears browser data

API:
localStorage.setItem(key, value)
localStorage.getItem(key)
localStorage.removeItem(key)
localStorage.clear()

Important:
localStorage only stores strings.

Objects must be converted using:
JSON.stringify()
JSON.parse()

Accessibility:
Accessible by any JavaScript running on the same domain.
Not accessible by the server directly.

Security reality:

* Vulnerable to XSS attacks
* No HttpOnly protection
* No Secure flag
* Any injected script can read it

Never store:

* Passwords
* Authentication tokens
* Sensitive personal data

Correct use cases:

* Theme preferences (dark/light)
* Shopping carts
* UI state
* Offline data
* Cached API responses

Rule:
Use localStorage only for non-sensitive client-side data.

────────────────────────────────

SESSIONSTORAGE

What sessionStorage is:
Same as localStorage, but with a shorter lifetime.

API:
Same as localStorage:
sessionStorage.setItem(key, value)
sessionStorage.getItem(key)

Size limit:
Same as localStorage (5–10 MB).

Lifetime:
Exists only for a single browser tab.
Destroyed when:

* The tab is closed
* The browser is closed

Each tab has its own sessionStorage.
Opening the same site in a new tab creates a new session.

Use cases:

* Multi-step forms
* Temporary UI state
* One-time session data
* Prevent data sharing between tabs

Do NOT use if:

* Data must persist across sessions
* User expects saved data

────────────────────────────────

COMPARISON SUMMARY

Cookie:

* Max size: ~4 KB
* Sent to server: Yes
* Lifetime: Configurable
* Security flags: Yes
* Best for: Authentication, sessions

localStorage:

* Max size: ~5-10 MB
* Sent to server: No
* Lifetime: Permanent
* Security flags: No
* Best for: UI state, carts, preferences

sessionStorage:

* Max size: ~5-10 MB
* Sent to server: No
* Lifetime: Per tab
* Security flags: No
* Best for: Temporary session data

────────────────────────────────

WHY JSON IS USED

localStorage and sessionStorage store only strings.

Wrong:
localStorage.setItem("cart", {a:1});

Correct:
localStorage.setItem("cart", JSON.stringify({a:1}));

Reading data:
JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem("cart"));

────────────────────────────────

COMMON BEGINNER MISTAKES

* Storing passwords in localStorage
* Storing JWT tokens in localStorage
* Forgetting JSON.parse
* Using sessionStorage when persistence is needed
* Assuming client-side storage is secure

────────────────────────────────

FINAL DECISION RULE

If the server needs the data automatically → Use Cookies
If data must persist after browser close → Use localStorage
If data should disappear when tab closes → Use sessionStorage